  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Trichoderma gelatinosum is a filamentous fungus belonging to the Hypocreaceae family, commonly found in New England forests and woodlands. This species plays an important ecological role as a decomposer and biocontrol agent against plant pathogens.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "Produces characteristic gelatinous or mucilaginous fruiting bodies that distinguish it from other Trichoderma species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Conidial morphology",
      "description": "Displays distinctive conidial arrangements under microscopy with specific spore shapes and phialide structures typical of the genus",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Colony appearance",
      "description": "Forms rapidly growing colonies on culture media with a greenish tint and woolly texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Trichoderma gelatinosum inhabits decaying wood, leaf litter, and soil in moist forest environments throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Trichoderma species like T. gelatinosum are used commercially as biological control agents and in biofertilizers because they promote plant growth and suppress harmful soil pathogens.",
